import{_ as o,C as t,o as a,c as s,E as r,a2 as c}from"./chunks/framework.BdWD-ld-.js";const v=JSON.parse('{"title":"Who checks the write after the first rejection?","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Who checks the write after the first rejection?","article_type":"comparative-study","edition":"research-center","research_question":"一次纠正性拦截与逐次绑定裁决在同轮重试中有什么不同？","summary":"Correcting an actor once and denying every unauthorized call are different contracts. Two separately authored sources and an internal state model locate the responsibility for retries without claiming deployed enforcement.","sources":"https://arxiv.org/html/2608.19861v2, https://github.com/microsoft/agent-governance-toolkit","date":"2026-11-28","column":"industry-architecture","category":"daily","pageClass":"runtime-v6-article","item_id":"SIG-20261128-001","lifecycle":"Published","cover":"/assets/covers/daily-2026-11-28-sig-20261128-001-cover.png","evidence_status":"Completed","citation_status":"Completed","editing_status":"Completed","publication_authorized":true},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/industry/2026-11-28-sig-20261128-001.md","filePath":"en/industry/2026-11-28-sig-20261128-001.md","lastUpdated":1795868712000}'),d={name:"en/industry/2026-11-28-sig-20261128-001.md"};function l(h,e,p,u,m,f){const n=t("ArticleCover"),i=t("ArticleTableScroll");return a(),s("div",null,[r(n,{image:"/assets/covers/daily-2026-11-28-sig-20261128-001-cover.png",kicker:"Research",title:"Who checks the write after the first rejection?",summary:"Correcting an actor once and denying every unauthorized call are different contracts. Two separately authored sources and an internal state model locate the responsibility for retries without claiming deployed enforcement.",version:"2026-11-28",languageHref:"/zh/industry/2026-11-28-sig-20261128-001",languageLabel:"中文"}),r(i,{language:"en"}),e[0]||(e[0]=c('<h1 id="who-checks-the-write-after-the-first-rejection" tabindex="-1">Who checks the write after the first rejection? <a class="header-anchor" href="#who-checks-the-write-after-the-first-rejection" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Who checks the write after the first rejection?&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A write is rejected, yet a second attempt in the same conversation turn proceeds. This is a strategy explicitly reported in the paper examined here, rather than an invented production incident: intervention disarms temporarily to give the actor room to recover. Platform implementers need to distinguish helping an actor correct its behavior from continuously preventing unauthorized side effects. Following the retry to its enforcement point is more revealing than checking only the first rejection.</p><h2 id="what-corrective-intervention-deliberately-leaves-open" tabindex="-1">What corrective intervention deliberately leaves open <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-corrective-intervention-deliberately-leaves-open" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What corrective intervention deliberately leaves open&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Section 3.4 of PolicyGuide describes an advisory mode that removes the first mutating call inconsistent with the current workflow state in a user-turn region and triggers a corrective verifier. Interception then disarms until the next user turn. This does not authorize or replay the rejected call; it keeps the execution loop moving.</p><p>The authors&#39; Appendix I.2 reports that forced same-turn retries, tool switches and retries after corrective-verifier exceptions were admitted. A new user turn rearmed interception, while repeated unauthorized transfers remained blocked. Different action classes therefore have different behavior. The presence of interception does not establish repeated checking of every call. <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2608.19861v2" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Paper and appendices</a></p><p>The complete-intervention-coverage theorem makes stronger assumptions: an ideal verifier gives a binding decision before commitment, and a rejected event cannot commit. Those assumptions cannot stand in for the evaluated probabilistic verifier and boundary schedule. This comparison concerns contracts; the theorem is not an implementation warranty.</p><h2 id="the-decision-core-leaves-work-for-the-host" tabindex="-1">The decision core leaves work for the host <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-decision-core-leaves-work-for-the-host" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The decision core leaves work for the host&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The examined Microsoft Agent Governance Toolkit policy-engine documentation states that the host supplies a complete snapshot for every call. The core retains no mutable state influencing later verdicts; the same manifest, snapshot, mode and dispatcher outputs produce the same verdict. Runtime failures deny and apply no transform. The host remains responsible for intercepting calls and enforcing the verdict. <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/agent-governance-toolkit/blob/main/policy-engine/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Policy-engine documentation</a></p><p>These are not competing entries in one experiment. The paper authors support the description of their corrective schedule; toolkit maintainers support a separately authored host contract. They are independent sources for their respective sides of a documentary comparison, without mutual reproduction or a shared safety or performance evaluation.</p><p>The toolkit also states that telemetry sink exceptions are swallowed. That detail makes the error boundary consequential: denying on a policy-core failure does not imply stopping every operation when logging fails. Calling every exception “fail closed” obscures which component actually prevents the side effect.</p><h2 id="an-internal-model-answers-one-retry-question" tabindex="-1">An internal model answers one retry question <a class="header-anchor" href="#an-internal-model-answers-one-retry-question" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;An internal model answers one retry question&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The completed same-day Analysis ran a hand-transcribed state model of the paper&#39;s schedule. Authorization remained false, interception started armed, and the script added 1, 3 or 10 same-turn retries. The first call was blocked; every added retry proceeded in the advisory model. A counterfactual that checked every call blocked them all. A new user turn rearmed interception; a verifier exception did not itself rearm it.</p><p>This is internal experimental evidence about the specified transitions. The persistent-checking control is a hypothetical implementation in the script, not the Microsoft toolkit. No PolicyGuide source, saved author checkpoint or full task benchmark was executed. The result supplies no model-success rate, latency, deployed-security measurement or product ranking. The <a href="/joinwell52/assets/evidence/2026-11-28-sig-20261128-001/experiment-evidence.json">internal model method and per-event outputs</a> retain inputs, controls and limitations.</p><p>The useful minimal sequence is rejection → same-turn retry → observation at the actual side-effect boundary. Testing only the first step makes the contracts look alike; the second exposes whether continuing mediation exists. This is our engineering interpretation of the bounded result, not a new end-to-end measurement.</p><h2 id="put-the-verification-question-where-effects-occur" tabindex="-1">Put the verification question where effects occur <a class="header-anchor" href="#put-the-verification-question-where-effects-occur" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Put the verification question where effects occur&quot;">​</a></h2><p>For systems needing continuous authorization, the following chain is a proposed review aid. It is not a unified process established as implemented by both projects.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Owner and input</th><th>Required output</th><th>Response if absent</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Prepare decision</td><td>Host collects target, parameters and current state</td><td>A snapshot valid for this call</td><td>Stop the call and complete the input</td></tr><tr><td>Decide</td><td>Policy core evaluates snapshot and rules</td><td>Explicit allow or deny</td><td>Apply the stated decision-boundary error policy</td></tr><tr><td>Commit effect</td><td>Host binds the verdict to the actual call</td><td>Denied calls cannot reach mutation</td><td>Record a bypass and stop admission</td></tr><tr><td>Retry and recover</td><td>Host rechecks state and authority</td><td>A valid decision for the retry</td><td>Do not reuse an invalidated permission</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The next evidence should identify real entry points and record each under initial rejection, same-turn retry, tool switching, verifier exceptions and new user turns. A core returning deny does not establish that the host mediated every entry point.</p><h2 id="the-costs-of-persistent-checks-remain-unmeasured" tabindex="-1">The costs of persistent checks remain unmeasured <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-costs-of-persistent-checks-remain-unmeasured" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The costs of persistent checks remain unmeasured&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Advisory correction has a reasonable purpose: preserve recovery and avoid repeatedly interrupting the same error. Persistent checking may introduce delay, false denial or liveness costs. A single-thread model with constant authorization cannot compare those costs or justify replacing a product.</p><p>Pinned PolicyGuide code and saved checkpoints are absent, as are a pinned toolkit host, adapters and real denial traces. Whether a concrete deployment continuously covers all retries therefore remains unresolved. Ask which action, call and exception each component must stop, then test the real entry points. The sources and internal model support the responsibility distinction, without establishing bypass-free deployment or general validity.</p><p>Source roles matter: paper results are author-reported, toolkit documentation describes its maintainers&#39; contract, the model is internal evidence, and the review chain is our interpretation and proposal. Publication, citation and implementation existence do not automatically establish independent validation. No Community Edition was generated because the available host evidence does not yet support a separate discussion angle for a named professional community.</p>',22))])}const g=o(d,[["render",l]]);export{v as __pageData,g as default};
